      <h2>Understanding Video Quality: 360p to 8K</h2>
      <p>
        Choosing the right quality for your download depends on your intended
        use and storage capacity. Here's a breakdown:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>360p:</strong> Low quality, very small file size. Suitable for older
          devices or when data is extremely limited. Expect visible pixelation on
          screens larger than a smartphone.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>480p (SD):</strong> Standard definition. Reasonable quality for
          older content or mobile viewing. Still relatively small files.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>720p (HD):</strong> High Definition. A good balance between quality
          and file size. Sharp on tablets and smaller monitors.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>1080p (Full HD):</strong> The most popular choice for most users.
          Excellent clarity on most TVs and monitors. File sizes are moderate.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>1440p (2K/QHD):</strong> A step above Full HD. Noticeably sharper
          on larger monitors (27" and above). Larger file sizes.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>2160p (4K/UHD):</strong> Ultra High Definition. Ideal for large
          4K TVs or professional editing. File sizes can be very large (several gigabytes
          for longer videos).
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>4320p (8K):</strong> The cutting edge. Only a few videos on YouTube
          are available in 8K. Extremely large files and requires powerful hardware
          for playback. Overkill for most users.
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p>When in doubt, 1080p offers the best compromise for most scenarios.</p>

      <h2>A Brief History of the MP4 Format</h2>
      <p>
        To truly appreciate why MP4 is the format of choice, a little history
        helps. MP4, formally known as MPEG-4 Part 14, was developed by the
        Moving Picture Experts Group (MPEG) and standardized in 2003. It was
        designed as a successor to older formats like AVI and QuickTime's MOV.
      </p>
      <p>
        The genius of MP4 lies in its container nature. It doesn't just hold
        video; it can encapsulate video streams (like H.264 or H.265), audio
        streams (like AAC), subtitles, and even metadata. This flexibility,
        combined with broad hardware and software support, made it the de facto
        standard for online video distribution. When you search for <strong
          >youtube to mp4 online</strong
        >, you're tapping into over two decades of engineering designed for
        universal compatibility.
      </p>

      <h2>MP4 vs. Other Formats</h2>
      <p>
        You might wonder why not just download in another format. Here's a quick
        comparison:
      </p>
      <h3>MP4 vs. WebM</h3>
      <p>
        WebM is an open, royalty-free format developed by Google. It's efficient
        and used heavily by YouTube internally. However, compatibility can be an
        issue on older devices or Apple products. MP4's H.264 codec has
        near-universal hardware decoding support.
      </p>
      <h3>MP4 vs. MKV</h3>
      <p>
        MKV (Matroska) is a highly flexible container that can hold almost any
        audio/video codec. It's popular for high-quality movie rips. However,
        MP4 enjoys better native support on mobile devices and smart TVs.
      </p>
      <h3>MP4 vs. AVI</h3>
      <p>
        AVI is an older Microsoft format. While it's still playable, it lacks
        modern features like efficient compression and subtitle embedding that
        MP4 offers. For online video, MP4 is unquestionably superior.
      </p>

